# 3. Record construct-and-access (bench/references.md §3), as a document.
#
#   mk   : Num -> Num -> ? = λx1:Num. λx2:Num. {x = x1, y = x2}
#   main : ? -> Num        = λp:?. p.x
#
# Through Phase 11 this was a pair and two positional projections, because a
# product was the only thing the language had that held two values: `getX`
# was `fst`, and nothing in the program recorded that component 0 was called
# `x`. Records arrived in Phase B2, so the fields have names now — one
# identity each, minted where the record is built and read by name where it
# is projected, in a different definition.
#
# The parameter and result types stay holes on purpose: a record type is a
# list of field identities, and identities are not spelled (DECISIONS.md,
# 2026-08-29). The constructor is where they are minted, which is what this
# program's first definition is for.

# mk : Num -> Num -> ? = λx1:Num. λx2:Num. {x = x1, y = x2}
rename-def mk
set-def-ann Num -> Num -> ?
construct-lam
move-parent
rename x1
set-ann Num
move-child 0
construct-lam
move-parent
rename x2
set-ann Num
move-child 0
construct-record
rename-field x
construct-var x1
add-field
rename-field y
construct-var x2

# main : ? -> Num = λp:?. p.x
create-definition
rename-def main
set-def-ann ? -> Num
construct-lam
move-parent
rename p
move-child 0
construct-field x
construct-var p
